pattern: quote a literal '[' inside a bracket expression

Inside a bracket expression, a '[' that does not start a character
class was copied to the regexp as is. An escaped character after it
is written without its backslash when regexp.QuoteMeta leaves it alone,
so an escaped ':' formed the start of a class in the regexp:

	Regexp(`[[\::]`, EntireString)  // "(?sU)^[[::]$"

regexp.Compile rejects that with "invalid character class range: `[::]`",
although Regexp documents that a nil error means the result compiles.
The literal '[' is now written as `\[`, which matches the same
characters and cannot start a class. FuzzRegexp found it; the input is
kept as a seed.

Reproducer, before this change: regexp.MustCompile(Regexp(`[[\::]`, 0))
panicked.

